# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DAJDA - example environment. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
# NEVER commit a real .env. No credential in this file is a working secret.
#
# The values below are development defaults. With NODE_ENV=production the app
# REFUSES TO START on any of them - see the guard in src/lib/env.ts. Each one
# fails silently otherwise: a mock provider hands out free subscriptions, the
# published mock secret lets anyone forge a webhook, and an http:// APP_URL
# drops the Secure flag from the session cookie.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string.
# The bundled docker-compose.yml serves exactly this URL.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://dajda:dajda@localhost:5432/dajda?schema=public"

# Secret used to sign session and CSRF tokens.
# Generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
AUTH_SECRET=

# Absolute public origin, used for cookie scoping, payment return URLs and the
# Origin check that backs CSRF protection.
# Production: must be your real https:// origin.
APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Which payment adapter to load: "mock" (development) or "flitt" (production).
# "mock" also enables /dev/checkout and the payment simulator, so production
# rejects it.
PAYMENT_PROVIDER="mock"

# Signing secret for the development mock provider's simulated webhooks.
# Only used when PAYMENT_PROVIDER=mock. The literal "dev-mock-secret" is
# rejected in production because it is published here.
MOCK_PAYMENT_SECRET="dev-mock-secret-not-for-production"

# Run a public deployment as an openly-labelled demo: "true" or "false".
#
# Set it when you want to show the app to somebody before there is a payment
# merchant account. It waives exactly two production checks - the payment
# provider and the mock webhook secret - and nothing else. APP_URL must still
# be https, because the session cookie's Secure flag depends on it.
#
# While it is on, every page carries a banner saying the data is invented and
# the payment is simulated. It cannot be combined with PAYMENT_PROVIDER=flitt.
DEMO_MODE="false"

# --- Flitt (https://docs.flitt.com) -----------------------------------------
# Required only when PAYMENT_PROVIDER=flitt.
FLITT_MERCHANT_ID=
FLITT_SECRET_KEY=
FLITT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Separate "credit" private key for payouts (P2P card credit). Optional:
# leave empty to run without payouts - checkout, subscriptions and refunds
# do not use it, and every payout attempt is refused while it is unset.
FLITT_CREDIT_KEY=
FLITT_API_URL="https://pay.flitt.com"

# --- Analyst earnings and payouts -------------------------------------------
# The analyst's percentage of a subscriber's payment; the rest is the
# platform's commission. Must match clause 5.3 of the signed agreement in
# docs/legal/agreement.md.
ANALYST_SHARE_PERCENT=85

# Smallest withdrawal, in tetri (2000 = 20 GEL). A smaller balance rolls into
# the next period rather than paying more in provider fees than it moves.
ANALYST_MIN_PAYOUT_MINOR=2000

# Publications required in EVERY whole week of the period for the payout
# activity check to pass. Weekly rather than monthly, so that steady delivery
# is what passes and a burst at the end of the month is not. A failing check
# does not block the request; it is shown to the administrator who releases
# the payout.
ANALYST_MIN_PUBLICATIONS_PER_WEEK=10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telegram (optional, two steps).
#
# Step 1 - "log in with Telegram" only. Needs the BotFather token, and the bot
# must point at this app's host: BotFather -> /setdomain -> the APP_URL origin.
# Without /setdomain Telegram refuses the login redirect.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN="123456:replace-me"
#
# Step 2 - bot notifications on top. The username is the bot's @name without
# the @, used to build the t.me deep link people press Start in. The webhook
# secret is any long random string, and it is the ONLY thing authenticating a
# webhook call: the bot's inbox refuses every request that does not carry it.
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME="DajdaBot"
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET="generate-a-long-random-string"
#
# Then register the webhook. This checks the token, refuses a username that
# belongs to a different bot, and prints Telegram's own last delivery error,
# which is the only way to find out that notifications quietly stopped:
#
#   npm run telegram:setup            register, then report
#   npm run telegram:setup -- --info  report only
#
# The token is a password for the bot. If it is ever pasted anywhere it should
# not be, BotFather -> /revoke issues a new one and kills the old.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email.
#
# "log" (the default) prints to the server console and sends nothing - with
# NODE_ENV=production the app refuses "log" unless DEMO_MODE=true. Two real
# adapters are built in, both HTTP APIs with free tiers; check their current
# limits before choosing, they change:
#
#   resend  - https://resend.com   (monthly allowance, generous per month)
#   brevo   - https://brevo.com    (daily allowance, better for steady traffic)
#
# NEITHER works without DNS. Verify the sending domain with the provider and
# add the SPF and DKIM records they give you, or mail is filed as spam no
# matter what this app does.
#
# EMAIL_FROM must be on that verified domain. A provider will refuse, or
# silently fail to deliver, a From address it has not been shown to own.
EMAIL_PROVIDER="log"
# EMAIL_API_KEY="re_..."
# EMAIL_FROM="DAJDA <no-reply@dajda.ge>"
#
# Check it before trusting it. This sends one real message through the same
# adapter the app uses, and prints the provider's own refusal if it fails:
#
#   npm run email:test -- you@example.com
#
# It proves the API call succeeded, not that the mail reached an inbox rather
# than a spam folder - only opening the mailbox shows that.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The outbox sweep: retries failed sends, and is the only thing that delivers
# notifications nobody is waiting on (a new bet, a settlement, a live notice).
# Point a scheduler at it every few minutes. Unset, the endpoint refuses
# everyone.
#
#   curl -H "authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" https://your-host/api/cron/notifications
#
# CRON_SECRET="generate-a-long-random-string"

# --- Seeding ----------------------------------------------------------------
# `npm run db:seed` upserts the sports and ONE administrator account, and
# nothing else. It deletes nothing and is safe to run twice: an administrator
# that already exists keeps its password. Set these to choose that account's
# credentials on first creation; leave SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD empty and one is
# generated and printed once.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@dajda.ge"
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Invented analysts, predictions and results are opt-in and belong only on a
# local machine: `npm run db:seed:demo`. Never on a public deployment - the
# platform's whole claim is that its published record is real.
#
# That command EMPTIES THE DATABASE first: every user, payment, prediction and
# audit entry. With NODE_ENV=production it refuses to run for that reason;
# SEED_ALLOW_DEMO_IN_PRODUCTION=1 overrides the refusal, and there is no good
# reason to set it on a database anyone cares about.
